            <ArticleTitle>Intraoperative diagnosis of bilateral coronary ostia stenosis: a rare case of ischemic heart disease in a 3-month-old patient.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1875-7</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.athoracsur.2011.04.039</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>We report a case of a 3-month-old child, presenting with signs of myocardial ischemia and hemodynamic instability, requiring extracorporeal membrane oxygenation support. Due to the unclear preoperative instrumental findings, a surgical exploration was performed revealing the presence of coronary ostia stenosis and an intramural course of both coronary arteries. The child underwent a successful plasty of the coronary artery ostia by resecting the excessive obstructing fibrous tissue followed by unroofing of the intramural coronary artery course with restoring of a normal myocardial perfusion and function.</AbstractText>
